HOW A RELEASE WORKS
Committing a manifest change on Main IS the rollout. The app fetches
firmware_manifest.json from Main and compares versions numerically.

The manifest has one entry per hardware type under "variants":
  "original" = original Super Switch (25.x firmware, V2_PCB_Super_Switch)
  "DJ"       = Super Switch DJ / Dual Jack (26.x firmware, Dual_Jack_PCB_Super_Switch)
App build 4.8.0+ reads only the entry matching the connected switch.
A "latestVersion" of "0" (or a missing entry) means "no OTA release yet"
for that hardware — the app will report up to date.

RELEASING (either switch type):
1. Export Compiled Binary in Arduino IDE (from the right sketch!).
2. Rename: SuperSwitch_vXX.X.ino.bin (original) or
           SuperSwitchDJ_vXX.X.ino.bin (Dual Jack).
3. Add the .bin to the "OTA Firmware Release" folder on Main.
4. Right click the new file's "Raw" button and copy the link.
5. In firmware_manifest.json, update ONLY that hardware's entry under
   "variants": latestVersion, downloadURL (the Raw link), releaseNotes
   (shown in the app).
6. Test: USB-flash one unit, then OTA-test one unit, before committing
   to Main.

ORIGINAL SUPER SWITCH ONLY — also update the legacy top-level fields
(latestVersion / downloadURL / releaseNotes) to match the "original"
entry. Older app versions still read them.

!! HARD RULES !!
- The legacy top-level "latestVersion" must ALWAYS stay below 26
  (below every Dual Jack version). Old app builds treat it as universal;
  breaking this rule would offer original-switch firmware to Dual Jacks
  on old apps. Firmware 25.10 / 26.5+ also refuse wrong-board updates
  (ERR_WRONG_BOARD), but old DJ firmware in the field may not.
- Version ranges are reserved: 25.x = original, 26.x = Dual Jack.
  Never cross them.
- Versions only go up. The app never offers a numerically lower version,
  so a "downgrade" release would strand devices.
